                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



with their skirts catching air. They were dancing in the Ballroom, in my
building, a floor under my feet and almost seventy years down, and
they weren’t doing it for me. They didn’t know I was there. They just
kept the floor.
   In the center of the floor, inlaid in the wood, was the same spiral and
triangle. The dancers stepped around it without looking, the way you
step around a crack you’ve known your whole life.
   I knew it. It was like the shape had been waiting inside me, and the
wall was just the first place it found to look out. I knew it the way you
know your mother’s knock.
   I was standing in the middle of my room with my mouth open
when the loop came in.
   I felt it before I heard it, through the riser, through the soles of my
feet: the eight seconds, coming up the pipe from outside the building.
Across the street, I thought, and I couldn’t have told you why. On the
raw feed, with nothing smoothing it, it was the loudest thing on the
block.
   And I watched what it did to the dancers.
   It didn’t stop the music. That’s what got me. The band kept playing,
the horns kept right on going. But one couple lost the step. Then the
couple next to them. A woman’s smile slid off her face like an egg off a
plate. A man put his hand on his partner’s back and just stood there
with it. One pair at a time, all the way down the floor, the shoulders
came down and the faces went flat and the shine went out of the wood,
until the band was playing to a hall full of people standing still in their
good clothes, and the spiral in the middle of the floor had gone as dark
as the boards around it. On the raw feed the color didn’t fade evenly. It
drained, all of it leaning one way, out through my window toward the
building across the street.
   Something clicked on the nightstand. The coin had lifted off the
wood, half an inch, and dropped back, and I was across the room with
my back against the door before I’d decided to move, the chair on its
side behind me. When I looked down, I was holding it. I didn’t remem‐
ber picking it up. It was hot now.




